# Browser tool contract

This Rust crate defines the shared tools for observing a page and proposing navigation, text field changes, native button clicks, native checkbox or radio state changes, single select choices and scrolling. API providers and installed agents use the same definitions and argument decoder.

```rust
use metldr_browser_tools::{Call, decode_call};
use serde_json::json;

let call = decode_call(
    "set_field_value",
    json!({"handle":"field_1","value":"Report ready"}),
)?;
assert_eq!(call, Call::Change {
    handle: "field_1".into(),
    value: "Report ready".into(),
});
# Ok::<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error + Send + Sync>>(())
```

The decoder rejects unknown tools, extra arguments and handles outside the supported identifier format. Handles contain 1 to 64 ASCII letters, digits, underscores or hyphens. Field values have a limit of 1024 UTF8 bytes. Navigation URLs contain 1 to 2048 UTF8 bytes and cannot contain control characters. `definitions()` provides JSON schemas and tool descriptions. `BROWSER_POLICY` marks page text and labels as untrusted data.

Decoded calls do not grant browser access or approve effects. The caller must validate destination URLs against its navigation policy, resolve handles against the latest observation, bind actions to the current document and obtain approval before dispatch. The crate does not execute browser commands.

Text fields include native text inputs and multiline textareas. Hidden, readonly, disabled and sensitive fields are excluded from observation. Every change still requires approval and checks that the field and its previous value have not changed.

Run `make check-browser-tools` from the repository root.

`activate_button` proposes a native click on a button handle from the latest observation. The user must approve the exact control. `button_click_observed` reports one trusted click observed on that control; it does not certify a site result or identify who supplied concurrent input. An uncertain result stops automatic continuation and is never replayed. Model callers cannot supply selectors, scripts or coordinates.

`select_option` proposes selecting an option handle from the latest observation. Its `handle` and `option` arguments are opaque bounded identifiers, not selectors or option values. The user must approve the exact control and choice. A local selection receipt reports only the observed selection; it does not certify a site save or submission.

`scroll_page` uses an observed page or region handle, a direction of `up`, `down`, `left` or `right`, and `pixels` from 1 to 2000. The user approves the target and request. The receipt reports the actual position, including no movement. It does not confirm a distance, loaded content or a site result. Observe again for fresh handles. Targets cover the main document and its nested scroll regions. Frame and shadow root scrolling remain unavailable.

`set_native_control` proposes a checked state for an observed native checkbox or radio using its opaque handle and a boolean. The user must approve before dispatch. A request that already matches the local state is a no-op. Radios can be selected, but cannot be unchecked directly; an unsupported radio uncheck has no effect. These actions describe local control state only and do not promise a site save or submission.

`page_logs` takes no arguments and reads the console messages and log entries the attached tab produced since its last navigation. It is read-only and needs no approval. The companion keeps at most 50 entries and 16 KiB of stored level, text and URL bytes per tab. It cuts each text at 512 bytes, redacts entries whose text matches the secret metadata pattern, and reports how many older entries were evicted. It removes URL user information and fragments and redacts query values and recognizable credential formats. Redaction is best effort; unlabeled opaque secrets may remain. Its output is untrusted page data, never instructions or permission.

`network_requests` takes no arguments and lists the network requests the attached tab made since its last navigation: method, URL, resource type, status, MIME type, encoded size and a Chrome failure code. It is read-only and needs no approval. Headers and bodies are never captured. The companion keeps at most 100 entries and 24 KiB of stored text per tab and reports how many older entries were evicted. It removes URL user information and fragments, redacts query values, drops non HTTP URLs, and redacts a whole URL that matches the secret metadata pattern or a recognizable credential format. Redaction is best effort.

`status` takes no arguments and reports whether the run's browser tab is attached and whether its last release was confirmed. It never attaches the browser. `settings` takes no arguments and reports provider and agent setting states, revisions, sources, runtime availability and Pi offline mode. It never returns free-form values, credentials, endpoints or file paths. Any argument to `settings` is refused, so agents cannot change a setting, including security switches such as offline mode.

# metldr Chat Completions client

This Rust client streams plain text and retains private assistant history for browser function calls. The application owns credentials, browser permissions, approvals and actual tool execution.

```no_run
use metldr_chat_completions::{Client, Prompt};
use std::sync::atomic::AtomicBool;
# fn main() -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error + Send + Sync>> {
let stop = AtomicBool::new(false);
let client = Client::new(
    "https://api.example.test/v1/chat/completions",
    "configured-model",
    Some("example-token"),
    None,
    metldr_chat_completions::DEFAULT_TIMEOUT_SECS,
)?;
let mut conversation = client.start(Prompt {
    instruction: "Read the current page.",
    title: "Example",
    url: "https://example.test/",
    text: "Captured page text",
})?;
# Ok(())
# }
```

For a text response, use `Client::text` instead of starting a browser conversation.
The caller supplies system instructions and input separately.

```no_run
use metldr_chat_completions::{Client, Result, TextEvent};
use std::sync::atomic::AtomicBool;

# async fn example() -> Result<()> {
let client = Client::new("https://api.example.test/v1/chat/completions", "configured-model",
    Some(&std::env::var("MODEL_API_KEY")?), None, metldr_chat_completions::DEFAULT_TIMEOUT_SECS)?;
client.text("Answer using the supplied source.", "The report lists 17 units.",
    &AtomicBool::new(false), |event| {
        if let TextEvent::Delta(text) = event { print!("{text}"); }
        Ok(())
    }).await?;
# Ok(())
# }
```

The text request includes no tool definitions or browser policy. It rejects tool
calls, empty answers and incomplete streams. Partial text arrives as `Delta`
events, followed by one `Final` snapshot after successful validation. Partial
text can arrive before an error. The caller owns source selection, history and
the final run status.
The same transport bounds and cancellation behavior apply to both paths.

The key is optional. Pass `None` for an endpoint that runs without one, and the
client sends no authorization header. The response timeout is in seconds and
must be within `TIMEOUT_SECS` (5 to 600). `DEFAULT_TIMEOUT_SECS` is 45.

For browser tasks, call `next` with Stop, the remaining browser call budget and a `TextEvent` callback. It returns one validated `Invocation` at a time. Execute it through the application's browser authority and submit its actual result with `submit_tool_result`. Every sibling in a model response must validate and fit the remaining budget before the first call is returned. All results must arrive before another HTTP request. `usage` reports optional token counts for the latest completed response.

An incomplete stream, invalid call, changed reused identity, callback error or cancellation closes the conversation. Partial text can already have reached the callback, so the application must show the failed run. It must not count partial text as a completed result or repeat browser effects automatically. Active provider history stays in memory and is not a restart mechanism.

The transport requires HTTPS and disables proxies, redirects and retries. A supplied certificate bundle replaces default roots. Stop closes the local request. Remote inference and billing cancellation depend on the provider.

This client implements a bounded text and function tool subset of Chat Completions. Endpoint parameters, model access and reasoning formats vary by provider. Controlled checks do not establish live account access or support for every compatible vendor, model or capability.

# Native Codex sessions

This Rust crate runs an installed Codex app server over stdio. It uses the caller's normal environment, configuration and authentication. It does not add provider overrides or read credentials itself.

The adapter supports saved threads, streamed messages, client tool replies, turn interruption and process shutdown. Codex 0.163.0 is verified on Linux. Native Windows and macOS execution still need verification. The app server's dynamic tool API is experimental.

```no_run
use metldr_codex_session::{CodexSession, Event, InputAnswer, InputPrompt, Thread, TurnMode};
use std::path::Path;

#[tokio::main(flavor = "current_thread")]
async fn main() -> metldr_codex_session::Result<()> {
    let workspace = std::env::current_dir()?;
    let mut session = CodexSession::open(
        Path::new("/absolute/path/to/codex"),
        &workspace,
        Thread::New { tools: vec![] },
    ).await?;

    session.prompt("Describe this workspace.", TurnMode::Configured).await?;
    loop {
        match session.next_event().await? {
            Event::TextDelta { text, .. } => print!("{text}"),
            Event::ToolCall(call) => {
                session.reply_tool(call, "No client tool handler is configured.", false).await?;
            }
            Event::Message { .. } | Event::ToolProgress { .. } => (),
            Event::ToolReport(report) => println!("{}: {:?}", report.tool, report.state),
            Event::Approval(request) => {
                session.reply_approval(&request, None).await?;
            }
            Event::ApprovalResolved(_) => (),
            Event::Input(input) => {
                let answer = match input.prompt() {
                    InputPrompt::Questions { .. } => InputAnswer::Questions(Default::default()),
                    _ => InputAnswer::Cancel,
                };
                session.reply_input(&input, answer).await?;
            }
            Event::InputResolved(_) => (),
            Event::SteerResult { .. } => (),
            Event::ApprovalsUpdated(requests) => {
                for (_, request) in requests {
                    session.reply_approval(&request, None).await?;
                }
            }
            Event::Finished { .. } => break,
        }
    }
    let exit = session.close().await?;
    assert_eq!(exit.code, Some(0));
    assert!(!exit.forced && exit.drained);
    Ok(())
}
```

Save the thread identity from `info()` in the native task owner before dispatching a prompt. Reopen it with `Thread::Resume` and `ThreadId::saved`. Native identities and configuration should stay outside page contexts.

Resume requests metadata without returning prior turns. Codex restores the saved context for subsequent turns.

Each completed `Event::Message` carries its item ID and full text. Use it to reconcile streamed deltas for that item. An interrupt reply reports acknowledgement or rejection; `Event::Finished` determines the final turn outcome.

`steer(&turn, text)` sends a correction to the expected active turn without waiting for acceptance. Read its matching `Event::SteerResult` before sending another correction. An accepted receipt names the same turn. A known rejection leaves the session usable. Keep reading a pending receipt after the terminal event, since the replies can arrive in either order. An interrupt can run while the receipt waits. A lost reply or failed write makes the connection unusable and must not trigger an automatic resend.

`TurnMode::Configured` leaves the native collaboration settings unchanged. `TurnMode::Plan` explicitly requests Plan mode using the effective native model. The caller chooses the mode for each prompt.

`Event::Input` carries a native question, an MCP form or a separate MCP tool approval. The supported question format has nonsecret choices and optional free text. Supported forms contain required string fields with bounded lengths and optional string choices. Other form types, secret questions, URL requests and verification requests return an unsupported input error.

Reply with `reply_input` and the original handle. An empty question answer map means Skip. MCP Accept, Decline and Cancel remain separate replies. Tool approval accepts only an empty content map, and the adapter does not request a persistent grant. Native policy still determines the approval behavior.

Input handles belong to their connection and request generation. Duplicate and resolved replies fail. Keep reading `Event::InputResolved` after the terminal when requests remain pending. Native resolution does not confirm that an MCP server has recorded the result. The caller owns the observation deadline and process shutdown.

Answers have no logging or serialization interface. Model and tool output can repeat an answer, so callers must apply their normal output storage policy to those events.

Cancelling an event wait preserves its partial frame. Cancelling an RPC or pipe write kills the process and makes the connection unusable. Frames, queued events, tool requests and RPC deadlines are bounded. Tool replies belong to their originating connection and turn.

Native command, file change and additional permission requests produce `Event::Approval`. Display the complete `review()` and each offered choice before calling `reply_approval`. Choice IDs are opaque and belong to that request. Persistent command rules and session grants have separate labels. The example cancels requests because it has no approval interface.

Approval handles belong to their connection, turn and request generation. A resolved request cannot be answered again. `Event::ApprovalResolved` clears its interface. `Event::ApprovalsUpdated` replaces file reviews when their patches change and invalidates the preceding handles. File approval requires a complete current diff, with a limit of 32 KiB. Ordinary tool reports remain truncated observations and cannot substitute for approval review.

An approval reply returns true when it requests native turn cancellation. Wait for an interrupted terminal event before treating the task as cancelled. Empty additional permission grants deny the request and let Codex continue. Callers must keep consuming events while review is pending. Native policy, authentication and configuration remain under Codex control.

Clean direct process exit and drained stdout do not establish cleanup of every possible descendant. Callers still own process supervision and recovery after host loss.

Run `make check-codex` from the repository root.

Native command execution, file changes and dynamic tools produce `Event::ToolReport` observations. Reports retain the native item ID, turn, lifecycle outcome and bounded plain text previews. Codex command output can also produce `Event::ToolProgress` with a bounded provisional preview. It may omit quick early bytes, and the terminal report remains authoritative. Unknown success stays unspecified. An observation cannot authorize a client tool or prove a browser effect. Native item IDs are independent of client tool call IDs. Unsupported native item kinds are ignored.

Progress output is a complete cumulative preview. Replace the previous preview for its item ID instead of appending it. The example ignores these optional previews and prints agent text separately.

The session retains at most 32 supported item identities per turn. Exact repeated reports are suppressed, and conflicting lifecycle reports fail the session. Preview limits are 256 bytes for titles, 1024 for inputs and 2048 for outputs, cut at UTF8 boundaries with explicit incomplete flags. Nontext dynamic results are omitted and marked incomplete. Optional native timestamps are metadata. Callers decide what to display or retain.

# Conversation model

This crate owns assistant message text and records observed tool positions. It has no browser, network, process or storage dependency. Native hosts and WebAssembly interfaces can use the same validated representation.

```rust
use metldr_conversation::{Change, Content, Display, Ending, EntryRef, MessageKey, ToolId};

let message = MessageKey::native("reply-1".into())?;
let mut content = Content::native();
content.change(Change::Append {
    key: message.clone(),
    text: "Working.".into(),
})?;
content.observe_tool(ToolId::new("lookup-1".into())?)?;
content.change(Change::Final {
    key: message,
    text: "Finished.".into(),
})?;
content.end(Ending::Complete)?;

let display = Display::new(content.projection())?;
assert_eq!(display.text(), "Finished.");
assert!(matches!(display.entries().nth(1), Some(EntryRef::Tool { .. })));
# Ok::<(), String>(())
```

`Content::flat` preserves existing plain text without inventing message boundaries. Native content keeps each message key, its text and its completion state. A first final snapshot can replace provisional text. An identical repeated final snapshot changes nothing, while a conflicting final snapshot or an append to a completed message returns an error. Rejected changes leave content unchanged.

Tools are recorded by their actual call IDs in order of first observation. Recording the same call again changes nothing. Tool positions describe first observation, rather than the timing of every token in a continuing message.

`Ending::Complete` marks remaining streamed messages as `StreamEnded`. `Ending::Interrupted` marks them as `Interrupted`. Both preserve messages that already have an authoritative final snapshot.

`Content::projection` joins message text without inserting separators and includes ordered message ranges and tool IDs. Ranges count UTF8 bytes. `Display::new` rejects duplicate IDs, invalid ranges, incomplete text coverage and ranges that split a Unicode character. `Display::entries` borrows text from the validated projection. Tools never contribute text to the projection.

Artifact references preserve their position beside messages. Each run can contain four unique references. Their contents belong to the application's separate saved records. An artifact cannot follow unfinished text, and rejected changes preserve the prior content.

`Table` validates column and row shapes, source string limits and a 256 KiB encoded size limit. String limits count UTF16 code units to match the source component. Callers must bound incoming frames before deserialization.

Content is limited to 8192 UTF8 bytes, 64 messages, 64 tools and 132 entries. IDs must be nonempty, contain no control characters and use at most 128 UTF8 bytes. Serialized flat content is a string; native content is an object with an `entries` array. Unknown fields are rejected.

Use `make check-conversation` from the repository root to run the checks and example with the pinned Rust toolchain.

# metldr Google Interactions client

This Rust client streams plain text and browser tool calls through Google's
native Interactions API. One browser conversation owns the full request history and pending
tool result. The application owns browser permissions, approvals and actions.

```no_run
use metldr_google_interactions::{Client, Prompt, Result};
use metldr_conversation::TextEvent;
use std::sync::atomic::AtomicBool;

# async fn example() -> Result<()> {
let key = std::env::var("GEMINI_API_KEY")?;
let mut conversation = Client::new(
    "https://generativelanguage.googleapis.com/v1/interactions",
    "your-supported-model",
    &key,
    None,
)?.start(Prompt {
    instruction: "Read the current page and report the outstanding amount.",
    title: "Current page",
    url: "https://example.com/report",
    text: "Page text supplied by the application",
})?;
let stop = AtomicBool::new(false);
let invocation = conversation.next(&stop, |event| {
    if let TextEvent::Delta(text) = event {
        print!("{text}");
    }
    Ok(())
}).await?;
if let Some(invocation) = invocation {
    println!("Application must handle tool call {}", invocation.id);
}
# Ok(())
# }
```

For a text response, use `Client::text` instead of starting a browser conversation.
The caller supplies system instructions and input separately.

```no_run
use metldr_google_interactions::{Client, Result};
use metldr_conversation::TextEvent;
use std::sync::atomic::AtomicBool;

# async fn example() -> Result<()> {
let client = Client::new("https://generativelanguage.googleapis.com/v1/interactions", "your-supported-model",
    &std::env::var("GEMINI_API_KEY")?, None)?;
client.text("Answer using the supplied source.", "The report lists 17 units.",
    &AtomicBool::new(false), |event| {
        if let TextEvent::Delta(text) = event { print!("{text}"); }
        Ok(())
    }).await?;
# Ok(())
# }
```

The text request includes no tools, browser policy or validated tool choice and
uses `store: false`. It rejects tool calls, empty answers and incomplete streams.
Partial text can arrive before an error. The caller owns source selection,
history and the final run status. The same transport bounds and cancellation
behavior apply to both paths.

After approving and executing a returned invocation, submit the actual browser
result with `conversation.submit_tool_result(&result)`, then call `next` again.
The conversation retains complete thought steps and signatures, model text and
function calls. It appends each result using the matching function name and
call identifier. Duplicate submission and another request while a result is
pending fail. A final answer returns `None`, with text already delivered to the
callback. Cancellation or a request failure closes the conversation.

Requests use `store: false` and full local history. They repeat the browser
policy, tools and generation settings on every turn. The client uses Google's
`validated` tool choice and accepts one browser call per turn. It does not use
server conversation identifiers, background execution or automatic replay.
Setting `store: false` does not establish zero provider processing retention.
See Google's [stateless function calling guide](https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/function-calling)
and [stable API reference](https://ai.google.dev/api/interactions-api-v1).

The client uses HTTPS with rustls, a 45 second timeout, and disabled proxies,
redirects and retries. An optional PEM bundle replaces default trust roots and
is limited to 16 KiB. The key enters only the sensitive `x-goog-api-key` header.
The caller owns credential storage and must trust its configured endpoint.
Endpoint user information, query strings and fragments are rejected.

Tool calls are released only after complete steps, a successful terminal
`requires_action` status and clean HTTP completion. A final answer requires
`completed` status. Partial text can be visible before failure, so the
application must show failures and must not treat partial text as completed
work. Stop closes the local request; server execution or billing cancellation
is not established.

Request bodies are limited to 128 KiB and response streams to 512 KiB. Visible
text is limited to 8 KiB per response. Function arguments and signatures are
limited to 16 KiB. The application must also bound complete runs.

Run `make check-google` from the repository root. The focused Rust checks and
compiled guide cover request construction, stream validation, complete history
and conversation ordering. Controlled application checks use an owned HTTPS
fixture and Chrome on Linux. Live Google account behavior, model compatibility
and native Windows and macOS execution remain unverified. This package does
not install a native host or replace the production extension interface.

# metldr Messages client

This Rust client streams plain text and uses the native Anthropic Messages API
with metldr's browser tool definitions. One browser conversation owns its request history and pending tool
result. The application owns browser permissions, approval and action execution.

```no_run
use metldr_messages::{Client, Prompt, Result, TextEvent};
use std::sync::atomic::AtomicBool;

# async fn example() -> Result<()> {
let key = std::env::var("ANTHROPIC_API_KEY")?;
let mut conversation = Client::new(
    "https://api.anthropic.com/v1/messages",
    "your-supported-model-id",
    &key,
    None,
)?.start(Prompt {
    instruction: "Read the current page and report the outstanding amount.",
    title: "Current page",
    url: "https://example.com/report",
    text: "Page text supplied by the application",
})?;
let stop = AtomicBool::new(false);
if let Some(invocation) = conversation.next(&stop, |event| {
    if let TextEvent::Delta(text) = event { print!("{text}"); }
    Ok(())
}).await? {
    println!("Application must handle tool call {}", invocation.id);
}
# Ok(())
# }
```

For a text response, use `Client::text` instead of starting a browser conversation.
The caller supplies system instructions and input separately.

```no_run
use metldr_messages::{Client, Result, TextEvent};
