```

The text request includes no tool definitions or browser policy. It rejects tool
calls, empty answers and incomplete streams. Partial text can arrive before an
error. A successful nonempty response ends with one `Final` snapshot after the
stream passes validation. The caller owns source selection, history and the
final run status.
The same transport bounds and cancellation behavior apply to both paths.

After approving and executing a returned browser invocation, submit the actual result
with `conversation.submit_tool_result(&result)`, then call `next` again. The
conversation preserves complete finalized assistant content, including opaque
thinking signatures and redacted thinking. It appends each matching tool result
once. A final answer returns `None`, with text already delivered to the callback.
A missing result prevents another request. Cancellation and failures close the
conversation without replay.

Requests use `stream: true`, the native `x-api-key` header and API version
`2023-06-01`. The configured model must match the response model identifier.
The caller owns credential storage and trusts its configured endpoint.
Bearer tokens, workload identity, managed tools, server fallback and thinking
configuration are outside this client interface. Returned thinking content is
preserved; the client does not enable thinking or verify its signatures.
See the official [streaming guide](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/streaming)
and [tool continuation guide](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/agents-and-tools/tool-use/handle-tool-calls).

Text arrives as incremental `Delta` events. A successful nonempty response ends
with one `Final` snapshot after complete validation. A tool call requires
complete content blocks,
consistent successful stop reason, `message_stop` and clean HTTP completion.
One browser call is accepted per turn. Unsupported content, incomplete output,
refusal, malformed streams and provider errors release no call. Partial text
can remain visible after failure, so the application must show the failure.
Unknown event types and pings are ignored within the event and byte limits.

The internal HTTPS client uses rustls, a 45 second timeout and disabled proxies,
redirects and retries. Optional PEM roots replace default roots and are limited
to 16 KiB. Request bodies are limited to 128 KiB and response streams to 512 KiB.
Visible text is limited to 8 KiB per response, with 16 KiB limits on block text,
function arguments and signatures. The application must also bound whole runs.
Stop closes local consumption and prevents returning a call; server computation
and billing cancellation are not established. Provider retention is separate
from local history, and a direct request does not establish zero retention.

Run `make check-messages` from the repository root. The Rust checks and compiled
guide cover request construction, incremental text, complete opaque history,
stream completion and conversation ordering. Live account and model behavior,
native Windows and macOS execution, and production extension installation
remain unverified.

# Native OpenCode sessions

The Rust crate runs an installed OpenCode executable with `acp` over stdio. It inherits the caller's environment, authentication and OpenCode configuration. OpenCode owns its native tools, model requests and saved sessions.

The adapter supports new sessions, saved session resume, streamed updates, native permission requests and cancellation. It uses ACP protocol 1, verified with OpenCode 2.0.32 on Linux through a controlled model provider. Native Windows, macOS and live provider behavior still need verification.

HTTP MCP attachment is verified across process restart with an owned Rust server, including authenticated requests, native tool execution and permission replies. Stdio declarations have source and boundary checks; installed stdio server execution remains unverified.

```no_run
use metldr_opencode_session::{Event, OpenCodeSession, Session};
use std::path::Path;

#[tokio::main(flavor = "current_thread")]
async fn main() -> metldr_opencode_session::Result<()> {
    let workspace = std::env::current_dir()?;
    let mut session = OpenCodeSession::open(
        Path::new("/absolute/path/to/opencode"),
        &workspace,
        Session::New,
        &[],
    ).await?;

    session.prompt("Describe this workspace.").await?;
    loop {
        match session.next_event().await? {
            Event::Update(update) => println!("{update}"),
            Event::Permission(request) => {
                session.reply_permission(&request, None).await?;
            }
            Event::Finished { stop_reason } => {
                println!("{stop_reason}");
                break;
            }
        }
    }
    let exit = session.close().await?;
    assert_eq!(exit.code, Some(0));
    assert!(!exit.forced && exit.drained);
    Ok(())
}
```

Prompts accept up to 128 KiB of UTF8 text, including the complete supplied context. Oversized prompts return an error without truncation.

Save the identity from `id()` before sending a prompt. Resume it with `Session::Resume` and `SessionId::saved` in the same workspace. Resume attaches the stored session without replaying its earlier messages or sending a prompt. Supply trusted `McpServer` declarations as the fourth argument, including again on resume. An empty list adds no MCP servers. HTTP headers and stdio environment values can contain credentials, so keep declarations in the native owner and out of page contexts and logs. OpenCode retains responsibility for connecting these servers and applying its native tool permissions.

`Event::Update` carries the native session update. The caller renders its text, tool status and configuration updates. A permission request exposes the native tool details and offered choices through `details()`. Pass an offered option ID to `reply_permission`, or `None` to cancel the request. The example cancels every request. Permission tokens belong to their connection and request, including when a native child task asks after a parent reply finishes. Invalid choices can be corrected, and answered tokens cannot be reused.

`cancel()` sends a cancellation notification. Wait for `Event::Finished` to establish the turn outcome. Cancelling an event wait preserves its partial frame. Cancelling an RPC or pipe write kills the direct process and prevents connection reuse. Frames, queued events, pending permission requests and RPC deadlines are bounded. Native errors during a prompt end the connection without assuming that its work completed.

`close()` detaches the native session when idle, closes stdin and waits for direct process exit and stdout drain. Saved history remains in OpenCode's database. Forced exit and incomplete drain are reported separately. Callers own descendant cleanup and supervision. The adapter does not sandbox OpenCode tools or connect the browser interface itself.

Run `make check-opencode` from the repository root.

# Native Pi sessions

This Rust crate runs an installed Pi executable in RPC mode over stdio. It uses the caller's environment and Pi configuration. The caller chooses the workspace, provider, model and session directory through `PiProfile`.

The transport sends JSON commands and receives JSON events. Pi owns its tools, Skills, authentication and saved session files. Resume a session by setting `PiProfile.resume` to its saved session file. Reopening a session does not itself send a prompt.

Set `PiProfile.mcp` to bind one temporary HTTP MCP server. The descriptor accepts only a named server on IPv4 loopback with a token of 64 hex characters. It creates a temporary extension registration for each session and passes the token only to the Pi child environment. The registration is removed after the child exits or the session is dropped. Each HTTP request asks for its connection to close. This does not add arbitrary Pi arguments or change Pi configuration.

```no_run
use metldr_pi_session::{PiProfile, PiSession, Result};
use serde_json::json;
use std::time::Duration;

fn run(profile: PiProfile) -> Result<()> {
    let mut session = PiSession::spawn(profile)?;
    session.send(json!({
        "id": "question",
        "type": "prompt",
        "message": "Describe this workspace."
    }))?;
    loop {
        let event = session.recv(Duration::from_secs(30))?;
        println!("{event}");
        if event["type"] == "agent_settled" {
            break;
        }
    }
    let exit = session.close()?;
    assert_eq!(exit.code, Some(0));
    assert!(!exit.forced && exit.drained);
    Ok(())
}
```

Use command IDs to match replies. An accepted `abort` reply acknowledges the request; wait for `agent_settled` before treating the turn as finished. A receive timeout leaves the session open.

Writes wait up to 250 milliseconds for the pipe writer. A failed or expired write kills the direct child and prevents session reuse. Delivery can be uncertain, so do not send the command again automatically. Run this transport outside an interaction thread and provide external process supervision. Commands are limited to 32 KiB, frames to 64 KiB, and session output to 1 MiB or 2048 records. Reaching an output limit ends the reader with an error.

`close` closes stdin, waits up to three seconds, then kills the direct child if necessary. It allows 200 milliseconds for pipe workers to finish. A false `drained` value means workers remain active, and repeated calls retain that state until the workers finish. Once the child is forcibly killed, later calls still report `forced` as true. The caller owns descendant cleanup. Dropping a session kills and waits for its direct child.

Pi 1.3.0 is verified on Linux with a controlled provider for Skills, native file reading, streaming, session resume and abort. Native Windows, macOS and live provider behavior still need verification. The adapter is a transport component and does not provide a sandbox for Pi tools.

Run `make check-pi` from the repository root.

# metldr Responses client

This Rust client streams text, browser tool calls, tables and generated answer
source through the OpenAI Responses protocol. Browser conversations keep request
history and finalized reasoning. Presentation conversations keep their tool
continuation in the client.
The application owns browser permissions, approvals and actions.

```no_run
use metldr_responses::{Client, Prompt, Result, TextEvent};
use std::sync::atomic::AtomicBool;

# async fn example() -> Result<()> {
let key = std::env::var("OPENAI_API_KEY")?;
let client = Client::new(
    "https://api.openai.com/v1/responses",
    "your-supported-model",
    &key,
    None,
)?;
let mut conversation = client.start(Prompt {
    instruction: "Read the current page and report the outstanding amount.",
    title: "Current page",
    url: "https://example.com/report",
    text: "Page text supplied by the application",
})?;
let stop = AtomicBool::new(false);
let invocation = conversation.next(&stop, |event| {
    if let TextEvent::Delta(text) = event { print!("{text}"); }
    Ok(())
}).await?;
if let Some(invocation) = invocation {
    println!("Application must handle tool call {}", invocation.id);
}
# Ok(())
# }
```

For a text response, use `Client::text` instead of starting a browser conversation.
The caller supplies system instructions and input separately.

```no_run
use metldr_responses::{Client, Result, TextEvent};
use std::sync::atomic::AtomicBool;

# async fn example() -> Result<()> {
let client = Client::new("https://api.openai.com/v1/responses", "your-supported-model",
    &std::env::var("OPENAI_API_KEY")?, None)?;
client.text("Answer using the supplied source.", "The report lists 17 units.",
    &AtomicBool::new(false), |event| {
        if let TextEvent::Delta(text) = event { print!("{text}"); }
        Ok(())
    }).await?;
# Ok(())
# }
```

The text request includes no tool definitions or browser policy and uses
`store: false`. It rejects tool calls, empty answers and incomplete streams.
Partial text can arrive before an error. The caller owns source selection,
history and the final run status. All modes share transport and cancellation
behavior, with mode-specific resource bounds.

After approving and executing a returned invocation, pass the actual browser
result to `conversation.submit_tool_result(&result)`, then call `next` again.
The conversation appends the finalized output and matching call identifier
before the result. A second submission or another request while a result is
pending fails. A final answer returns `None`, after its ordered text events
reach the callback. Cancellation or a request failure closes the conversation.
Partial text can be visible before a failure, so the application must show the
failure and must not treat partial text as a completed task.

The client uses HTTPS with rustls, a 45 second timeout, and disabled proxies,
redirects and retries. An optional PEM bundle replaces the default trust roots
and is limited to 16 KiB. Credentials enter only the sensitive Authorization
header. Endpoint user information, query strings and fragments are rejected.
The caller owns credential storage and must trust its configured endpoint.

Requests use `store: false`, encrypted reasoning, strict tool schemas and one
tool call at a time. Presentation mode offers `render_a2ui` for
`copilotkit://open-generative-ui-tables`, with one root `Table` component,
and `generateSandboxedUi` for HTML, CSS and JavaScript answer source.
Its `next` method accepts separate text and presentation progress callbacks.
Progress includes only complete JSON values and remains provisional until the
request completes. The caller must save each accepted update and isolate any
generated code from application permissions. A callback error closes the
conversation. Completed source is returned as `PresentationContent::Generated`.
The client validates event ordering, identifiers, output
items and terminal output, then requires clean HTTP completion before returning
a tool call. It retains finalized opaque reasoning for the next request.
Browser and text requests remain limited to 128 KiB, 16 KiB per tool argument,
128 KiB per SSE line/event, and 512 KiB per stream. Presentation requests are
limited to 2 MiB, tool arguments to 384 KiB, SSE lines/events to 1 MiB, and
streams to 8 MiB. Text is limited to 8 KiB per response. The application must
also bound complete runs. Generated source has a combined 256 KiB limit and
at most 1024 fragments in each ordered section.

Browser and text responses allow at most 4096 events. Presentation responses
allow at most 65536 events.

Run `make check-responses` from the repository root. The compiled guide and
focused Rust checks cover request construction, streaming, finalized history
and conversation ordering. The application verification uses a controlled HTTPS
fixture and Chrome on Linux. Live provider behavior and native Windows and
macOS execution remain unverified. This package does not install a native host
or replace the production extension interface.
